WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.362
Your notes describe the orbit's orientation with a frame that rides on the orbit.

00:00:05.362 --> 00:00:07.480
It's called the perifocal frame.

00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:13.751
p hat points from the focus to the periapsis, the same direction as the eccentricity vector.

00:00:13.751 --> 00:00:19.215
q hat is ninety degrees ahead of it, in the orbit plane, in the direction of motion.

00:00:19.215 --> 00:00:22.727
And h hat sticks out of the plane, completing the set.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.465
In this frame the orbit is simple.

00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:33.440
The satellite sits at r cosine theta along p hat, and r sine theta along q hat, with nothing out of the plane.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:42.613
So all of the three dimensional information lives in one question: how are p hat, q hat and h hat pointed in the E C I frame?

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.427
That's exactly what a direction cosine matrix stores.

00:00:47.427 --> 00:00:53.276
Write p hat, q hat and h hat in their I, J, K components, and stack them as rows.

00:00:53.276 --> 00:00:54.720
That stack is C P N.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:03.200
Each entry is a dot product, the cosine of the angle between one perifocal axis and one E C I axis.

00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:15.493
Because we built the orientation with three turns, Omega about K, then i about the node line, then omega about h, the matrix is a product of three simple rotations.

00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:25.127
C P N equals C three of little omega, times C one of i, times C three of capital Omega.

00:01:25.127 --> 00:01:34.487
Read it right to left: first the Omega turn, then the tilt, then the spin in the plane.

00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:39.892
These are the same elementary rotations from the kinematics lessons.

00:01:39.892 --> 00:01:42.478
C three turns about the third axis.

00:01:42.478 --> 00:01:44.547
C one turns about the first.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.601
We don't need all nine entries.

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:50.380
Start with the third row, which is h hat.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.424
The third row of C three of omega is just zero, zero, one.

00:01:56.424 --> 00:02:04.480
So the third row of the whole product is the third row of C one of i, times C three of Omega.

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:09.545
Multiply that row into C three of Omega.

00:02:09.545 --> 00:02:15.773
The result is sine i sine Omega, minus sine i cosine Omega, and cosine i.

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:18.460
Check it with the picture.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:24.813
h hat leans away from K by the angle i, so its K component is cosine i.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.854
Now the third column.

00:02:26.854 --> 00:02:31.740
Multiply C P N by the unit vector along K, working from the right.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:38.260
That gives sine omega sine i, cosine omega sine i, and cosine i.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.041
Now reverse it.

00:02:42.041 --> 00:02:45.884
The corner entry C three three is cosine i.

00:02:45.884 --> 00:02:49.460
So i is the arc cosine of C three three.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.800
Arc cosine always returns an angle from zero to one hundred eighty degrees.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.400
That's exactly the range of inclination, so no extra check is needed.

00:02:59.733 --> 00:03:00.658
Next, Omega.

00:03:00.658 --> 00:03:06.750
C three one is sine i sine Omega, and minus C three two is sine i cosine Omega.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:12.533
Since sine i is never negative, this pair points in the direction of Omega.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:20.800
So Omega is atan two of C three one and minus C three two.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.487
Why atan two and not arc cosine?

00:03:23.487 --> 00:03:26.678
Arc cosine can only give zero to one eighty.

00:03:26.678 --> 00:03:31.174
It can't tell one forty five degrees from two hundred fifteen.

00:03:31.174 --> 00:03:37.047
atan two looks at the signs of both entries, so it lands in the correct quadrant.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.980
The same reasoning for the third column gives little omega equals atan two of C one three and C two three.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:48.043
Two practical notes.

00:03:48.043 --> 00:03:55.501
atan two returns minus one eighty to one eighty, so add three sixty to a negative answer.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:04:03.127
And if i is zero, both pairs are zero over zero: that's the equatorial orbit, with no node.
